  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic** with confidence 0.75. The test results were very noisy, so this isn't a certain call.\n\n**Design:** I picked two test tasks that pull in opposite directions:\n- **L2-S1-M1:** every rule in it had already been trained, but its responses matched those of the trained tasks on at most 37.5% of inputs (0% at first).\n- **L1-S1-M2:** it has one rule (M2) the learner had never been trained on, but its responses match a trained task on 75% of inputs.\n\nA part-based learner should learn L2-S1-M1 faster; a holistic learner should learn L1-S1-M2 faster.\n\n**Results:**\n\n| Stage | Training so far | Examples needed (mean) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 2 tasks, each rule seen once | L2-S1-M1 \u2248 33 (n=4); L1-S1-M2 \u2248 15 (n=10 across stages); new low-overlap control \u2248 25 (n=3) |\n| 2 | L2, S1, M1 each in 2 trained tasks | L2-S1-M1 \u2248 28 (n=8) |\n| 3 | L2, S1, M1 each in 3 trained tasks | L2-S1-M1 \u2248 27 (n=12) |\n\n**Reading:**\n- **Not part-based:** overlap predicted the cost and new rules didn't. The task built only from known rules cost about as much as the new control task.\n- **Not factor-when-justified:** once the rules had appeared in 2 tasks, and then 3, a learner of that type (k=2 or k=3) should have learned L2-S1-M1 much faster. It didn't. The small drop fits its overlap with trained tasks rising from 0 to 37.5%.\n\n**Caveat:** single test results varied hugely, from 1 to 61 examples. A modest effect from reusing rules could be hidden in that noise. That uncertainty is why the confidence is 0.75 rather than higher. I used 41 of the 60 budget points.",
